WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:30.000
Dark Factory explores governed autonomous software delivery.
This is a recorded still-image walkthrough, not a live customer deployment.

00:30.000 --> 00:45.000
Platform teams need faster delivery without losing control.
Start with a request, acceptance checks, constraints and recovery ownership.

00:45.000 --> 01:15.000
The control room shows service posture and operational context.
Its metrics and costs are synthetic examples, not customer results.

01:15.000 --> 01:45.000
The synthetic goal illustrates the contract, not submitted authorization.
An owner must authorize the actual goal before live work proceeds.

01:45.000 --> 02:15.000
Run: npm run demo -- --scenario normal-feature
The recorded report shows simulated production-ready evidence, not certification.

02:15.000 --> 02:45.000
The offline report passed all ten scenarios and idempotent replay.
No GitHub or Azure service was contacted. Dashboard fixtures stay unchanged.

02:45.000 --> 03:15.000
Run: npm run demo -- --scenario deployment-regression
The simulated regression restores traffic and opens one deduplicated incident.

03:15.000 --> 03:45.000
Budget exhaustion stops mutation; it does not authorize unlimited retries.
Independent review and deterministic checks remain separate controls.

03:45.000 --> 04:00.000
Inspect audit correlation: goal, PR, commit, run, deployment and trace.
These dashboard identifiers are illustrative, not live scenario updates.

04:00.000 --> 04:30.000
GitHub is the control plane; Azure is the configured runtime plane.
Live telemetry requires an adapter; live automation needs explicit configuration.

04:30.000 --> 05:00.000
Bring one reversible non-production request and its owners.
Agree evidence, permissions, a baseline and rollback before beginning a pilot.
